(window.__PQ=window.__PQ||{})["02-sdlc-agile-devops"]={"slug":"02-sdlc-agile-devops","title":"SDLC, Agile and DevOps","accent":"#34d399","sections":[{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases","title":"SDLC phases","c":[{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases-c1","t":"Why a life cycle exists","p":"Building software without stages means building before you know what is wanted, testing after it is too late, and releasing without a plan. A life cycle gives work a shape: decide, build, check, release, run.","k":[["Why is a defect cheaper to fix early?","Fewer people, artifacts and customers depend on the wrong decision, so less has to be undone."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases-c2","t":"Planning and requirements","p":"Planning decides whether to do the work and what the outcome should be. Requirements describe what the software must do (functional) and how well it must do it (non-functional).","k":[["Give a non-functional requirement for a login API.","For example, p99 latency under 500 ms and 99.95 percent availability, measured monthly."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases-c3","t":"Design and architecture","p":"Design decides how the system will meet the requirements: components, data, interfaces, hosting and security. Architecture is the set of design decisions that are expensive to change later.","k":[["What goes into an ADR besides the decision?","The context that forced the choice and the consequences, good and bad."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases-c4","t":"Implementation","p":"Implementation is writing, reviewing and integrating code. In a modern flow it includes the build, tests and review as part of the same activity.","k":[["Why keep pull requests small?","They are reviewed faster and more carefully, merge with fewer conflicts and are easy to roll back."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases-c5","t":"Testing and verification","p":"Testing checks that the software meets the requirements and does not break what already worked. It happens at several levels and, ideally, on every change.","k":[["Which is validation, a unit test or a user acceptance session?","The user acceptance session, because it checks the product against the real need."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases-c6","t":"Deployment and release","p":"Deployment puts a build into an environment. Release makes its features available to users. They can happen at different times.","k":[["Why promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding per environment?","A rebuild can produce different bytes, so what you tested is no longer what you ship."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-sdlc-phases-c7","t":"Operations, maintenance and retirement","p":"After release the software runs, is monitored, patched, extended and eventually retired. This is usually the longest and most expensive phase.","k":[["Name the four kinds of maintenance.","Corrective, adaptive, perfective and preventive."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s02-sdlc-artifacts","title":"SDLC artifacts and governance","c":[{"id":"s02-sdlc-artifacts-c1","t":"Artifacts by phase","p":"Each phase leaves behind evidence and inputs for the next one. Knowing them tells you what \"done\" means for a phase.","k":[["Which artifact would you open first at 3 a.m. during an alert?","The runbook linked from that alert."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-sdlc-artifacts-c2","t":"User stories and acceptance criteria","p":"A user story states a need from the user's side: \"As a customer, I want to see my order status, so that I know when it arrives.\" Acceptance criteria are the checks that prove it is done.","k":[["Why write criteria as Given, When, Then?","They are concrete, unambiguous and can become automated tests with little translation."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-sdlc-artifacts-c3","t":"Definition of ready and definition of done","p":"The definition of ready says when a work item is clear enough to start. The definition of done says what must be true before it counts as finished. Both are short team agreements.","k":[["Who owns the definition of done?","The whole team, agreed and revisited in retrospectives."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-sdlc-artifacts-c4","t":"Traceability from requirement to production","p":"Traceability means you can follow a requirement to the code that implements it, the tests that check it and the release that shipped it, and back.","k":[["How do you link a commit to a ticket cheaply?","Put the ticket ID in the commit message or branch name and enforce it with a hook or CI check."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-sdlc-artifacts-c5","t":"Change management in a pipeline","p":"Change management is how an organisation decides, records and approves changes to production. DevOps replaces slow manual boards with automated, evidence-based controls for routine changes.","k":[["Why can automated checks beat a change board?","They inspect the actual change on every deployment and leave a record, instead of relying on a description."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-sdlc-artifacts-c6","t":"Documentation that stays alive","p":"Documentation is useful when it is easy to find, short and updated with the change. Docs separate from the code usually rot.","k":[["Where should the runbook for a service live?","In the service's repository, versioned and reviewed with the code it describes."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s03-sequential-models","title":"Plan-driven SDLC models","c":[{"id":"s03-sequential-models-c1","t":"Waterfall","p":"Waterfall runs the phases once, in order: requirements, design, build, test, deploy, maintain. Each phase finishes and is signed off before the next begins.","k":[["What is the main weakness of waterfall for software?","User feedback and testing come late, when changes are most costly."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-sequential-models-c2","t":"V-model","p":"The V-model is waterfall with testing planned in parallel. Every design phase on the left of the V has a matching test phase on the right.","k":[["What does the V-model add to waterfall?","Test activities defined alongside each design phase, with traceability."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-sequential-models-c3","t":"Incremental and iterative development","p":"Incremental: build the product in slices, each slice working and releasable. Iterative: build a rough version, then refine it repeatedly. Most modern methods do both.","k":[["What is a vertical slice?","A thin feature that cuts through every layer, from UI to data, and works end to end."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-sequential-models-c4","t":"Spiral model","p":"The spiral model repeats four steps in loops: set objectives, analyse risks, build and test, and plan the next loop. Each loop starts with the biggest risk.","k":[["What does the spiral model do first in each loop?","Look at objectives and then the riskiest unknown, so risk drives the plan."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-sequential-models-c5","t":"Prototyping and RAD","p":"Prototyping builds a quick, throwaway or evolving model to show users and learn what is really needed. Rapid Application Development (RAD) combines that with short builds and heavy user involvement.","k":[["Why is a throwaway prototype risky?","Management may ask to ship it, carrying untested shortcuts into production."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-sequential-models-c6","t":"Choosing a plan-driven model","p":"No model is best. The right one depends on how well requirements are known, how costly change is and what rules apply.","k":[],"o":[["Waterfall or V-model","scope fixed by contract or regulation and well understood","users still discovering what they need"],["Spiral or risk-first","large, costly project with major unknowns","small, familiar changes"],["Agile or DevOps","product evolves with feedback and can release often","physical product cannot be changed after shipping"]]}]},{"id":"s04-agile-and-devops-models","title":"Agile, Lean and DevOps as SDLC models","c":[{"id":"s04-agile-and-devops-models-c1","t":"The Agile Manifesto","p":"In 2001 a group of practitioners wrote four value statements and twelve principles for software work. The key idea is to deliver working software in small steps and adjust based on feedback.","k":[["Does the Manifesto reject documentation?","No, it values working software more than comprehensive documentation."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-agile-and-devops-models-c2","t":"Lean thinking and waste","p":"Lean comes from manufacturing (Toyota). It asks you to deliver value to the customer as fast as possible by finding and removing waste, and by making work flow smoothly.","k":[["Name two ways to improve flow without working harder.","Limit work in progress and shrink waiting times such as review and approval queues."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-agile-and-devops-models-c3","t":"Agile delivery frameworks at a glance","p":"Agile is a set of values. Frameworks are concrete ways to apply them. The common ones are Scrum, Kanban, Extreme Programming (XP) and scaling approaches such as SAFe.","k":[],"o":[["Scrum","cross-functional team with a product owner and planned increments","work is mostly unplanned interrupts like support"],["Kanban","continuous flow, operations or platform work","you need fixed commitments for a date"],["XP practices","any team that wants better code quality","no one can commit time to automated testing"]]},{"id":"s04-agile-and-devops-models-c4","t":"DevOps as an extension of agile","p":"Agile helped teams build software in short cycles, but the finished work often waited weeks for operations to release it. DevOps removes that wall by sharing responsibility from commit to production.","k":[["What does DevOps add to agile?","Automation and shared ownership across build, release and operation, so feedback comes from production."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-agile-and-devops-models-c5","t":"Shift left and continuous everything","p":"Shift left means moving checks such as testing, security and operability review earlier, to where they are cheaper. \"Continuous\" practices run those checks on every change.","k":[["Difference between continuous delivery and continuous deployment?","Delivery keeps every change releasable with a manual release decision; deployment releases automatically."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-agile-and-devops-models-c6","t":"DevSecOps and platform engineering","p":"DevSecOps builds security checks into the pipeline instead of reviewing at the end. Platform engineering gives product teams a ready-made, self-service path to build, deploy and run, so each team does not rebuild the same tooling.","k":[["What problem does platform engineering solve?","It reduces the cognitive load and duplication of every team assembling its own delivery tooling."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s05-scrum","title":"Scrum in practice","c":[{"id":"s05-scrum-c1","t":"Scrum roles","p":"Scrum has three accountabilities: the Product Owner decides what is most valuable, the Developers (everyone needed to build and ship) decide how, and the Scrum Master helps the team follow the process and removes obstacles.","k":[["Who decides how the work is done in a sprint?","The Developers, as a self-managing team."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-scrum-c2","t":"The sprint and its events","p":"A sprint is a fixed period, one to four weeks, that produces a usable increment. Inside it are five events: the sprint itself, planning, daily scrum, review and retrospective.","k":[["What is a sprint goal for?","It gives the team one coherent purpose, so scope can flex without losing the point of the sprint."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-scrum-c3","t":"The product backlog and refinement","p":"The product backlog is the ordered list of everything that might be needed. Refinement is the ongoing activity of making upcoming items clear, small and estimated.","k":[["Why is the bottom of the backlog kept rough?","Details go stale and priorities change, so detailed work is done only when it is near."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-scrum-c4","t":"Increment, Definition of Done and the Sprint Review","p":"An increment is a usable, finished step toward the product goal. Everything in it meets the Definition of Done, and each sprint adds to earlier increments.","k":[["Can a team release in the middle of a sprint?","Yes, Scrum does not require waiting for the review to release."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-scrum-c5","t":"Retrospectives that change something","p":"The retrospective is a regular meeting where the team looks at how it works and picks concrete improvements for the next sprint.","k":[["What makes a retrospective action good?","It is specific, has one owner and appears in the backlog with a date."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-scrum-c6","t":"Scrum anti-patterns","p":"Anti-patterns are practices that look like Scrum but defeat its purpose. Recognising them is half of fixing them.","k":[],"o":[["Scrum","planned product work with a stable team and fixed cadence","most work arrives unplanned and must start immediately"],["Kanban","support, on-call and platform requests that arrive continuously","you need a fixed delivery commitment to a date"],["Scrumban","mix of planned work and interrupts","the team wants strict sprint commitments only"]]}]},{"id":"s06-kanban-and-xp","title":"Kanban and Extreme Programming","c":[{"id":"s06-kanban-and-xp-c1","t":"Kanban boards and WIP limits","p":"A Kanban board shows each work item as a card moving through columns such as To do, In progress, Review, Done. A WIP limit caps how many cards a column may hold.","k":[["What should you do when the Review column is at its WIP limit?","Review something to unblock the flow rather than starting new work."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-kanban-and-xp-c2","t":"Flow metrics","p":"Flow metrics describe how work moves: how long it takes, how much finishes, how much is in progress and how old items are. They come from the board and need no estimates.","k":[["Why prefer the 85th percentile to the mean?","It gives a promise you can keep most of the time and is not distorted by a few very slow items."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-kanban-and-xp-c3","t":"Cumulative flow and bottlenecks","p":"A cumulative flow diagram (CFD) stacks the count of items in each state over time. The vertical thickness of a band is the WIP in that state; the horizontal distance is the delay.","k":[["What does a widening band in a CFD tell you?","Work is entering that state faster than it leaves, so it is a bottleneck."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-kanban-and-xp-c4","t":"Extreme Programming practices","p":"Extreme Programming (XP) is a set of engineering practices that keep code easy to change: test first, integrate continuously, refactor, pair and keep designs simple.","k":[["Why refactor only when tests are green?","Passing tests tell you quickly if the cleanup changed behaviour."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-kanban-and-xp-c5","t":"Trunk-based development and pairing","p":"Trunk-based development means everyone commits small changes to one main branch at least daily, using flags to hide unfinished features. Pairing and mobbing are ways to review and share knowledge as the code is written.","k":[["How can unfinished code live safely on main?","Behind a feature flag that is off by default."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-kanban-and-xp-c6","t":"Technical debt","p":"Technical debt is the extra future cost created by shortcuts in code, tests, infrastructure or documentation. Like financial debt, it accrues interest: every change becomes slower.","k":[["Name one way to keep technical debt visible.","Track it as backlog items with cost estimates and review the trend regularly."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s07-estimation-planning-metrics","title":"Estimation, planning and agile metrics","c":[{"id":"s07-estimation-planning-metrics-c1","t":"Relative estimation and story points","p":"Instead of guessing hours, teams compare items: \"this story is about twice as hard as that one\". The unit is a story point, a made-up number that only means something inside one team.","k":[["Why compare velocity only within a team?","Points are relative to each team's own scale, so cross-team comparison is meaningless."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-estimation-planning-metrics-c2","t":"Forecasting with throughput","p":"Throughput forecasting uses the count of finished items per week from history and runs many simulated futures (Monte Carlo) to give a range of dates with probabilities.","k":[["What is the benefit of quoting the 85th percentile?","It states a date you are likely to hit, rather than a date you hit half the time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-estimation-planning-metrics-c3","t":"Roadmaps and release planning","p":"A roadmap shows direction over months: outcomes the product aims at, not a list of dated features. Release planning turns the next slice of that direction into scheduled increments.","k":[["Why should the Later column carry no dates?","The work is not yet understood, so dates would be guesses that become expectations."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-estimation-planning-metrics-c4","t":"OKRs and outcome measures","p":"Objectives and Key Results (OKRs) set an ambitious qualitative goal and a few measurable results to show progress. They link team work to outcomes.","k":[["Which is the outcome, deploys per week or customers completing checkout faster?","Customers completing checkout faster; deploys are an output."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-estimation-planning-metrics-c5","t":"Metrics that help and metrics that hurt","p":"A metric used as a target stops being a good metric (Goodhart's law). Pick measures that guide improvement and cannot be gamed cheaply.","k":[["Why pair a speed metric with a stability metric?","Otherwise teams can look fast by shipping broken changes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-estimation-planning-metrics-c6","t":"Capacity, interrupts and sustainable pace","p":"Teams have limited focused time. Support requests, meetings and on-call reduce it. Planning must include them or commitments fail.","k":[["Why not plan to 100 percent capacity?","Unplanned work always arrives, and a plan with no slack fails the moment it does."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s08-devops-culture-calms","title":"DevOps culture and CALMS","c":[{"id":"s08-devops-culture-calms-c1","t":"The wall of confusion","p":"Traditionally, developers were rewarded for shipping features and operations for keeping things stable. Each side handed work to the other over a \"wall\", and each optimised for its own measure.","k":[["Why does the wall make releases riskier?","It discourages frequent deploys, so changes batch up and each release becomes large and hard to diagnose."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-devops-culture-calms-c2","t":"CALMS","p":"CALMS is a checklist of five areas a DevOps change touches: Culture, Automation, Lean, Measurement and Sharing.","k":[["Which CALMS letter do tools mainly address?","A, Automation. The other four are about people and process."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-devops-culture-calms-c3","t":"You build it, you run it","p":"The team that writes a service also carries its pager, watches its dashboards and fixes its incidents. Running software teaches design lessons that no review can.","k":[["What support must exist for you-build-it-you-run-it to work?","A good platform, sensible on-call, useful alerting and time to fix operability problems."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-devops-culture-calms-c4","t":"Blameless culture and psychological safety","p":"When something fails, a blameless culture asks how the system allowed a mistake, not who made it. People report problems early when they are not punished for them.","k":[["Does blameless mean no accountability?","No. Accountability moves to fixing the system and following through on actions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-devops-culture-calms-c5","t":"Team topologies and collaboration modes","p":"Team Topologies describes four team types and three ways teams interact so that work flows with minimal hand-offs.","k":[["What reduces queues between product teams and infrastructure?","A platform team offering self-service capabilities instead of ticket-based requests."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-devops-culture-calms-c6","t":"DevOps adoption anti-patterns","p":"Patterns that look like DevOps but keep the old problems.","k":[["Why start with one value stream?","You learn cheaply, show measured results and build credibility for wider change."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s09-three-ways-value-streams","title":"The Three Ways and value streams","c":[{"id":"s09-three-ways-value-streams-c1","t":"The First Way - flow","p":"Make work move from idea to customer faster, in a smooth stream, by making it visible, limiting work in progress and reducing the size of each batch.","k":[["Why does a smaller batch speed up learning?","Feedback on each change arrives sooner and problems are easier to trace to a single cause."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-three-ways-value-streams-c2","t":"The Second Way - feedback","p":"Create fast, constant feedback from right to left: from production and customers back to developers, so problems are found and fixed where they start.","k":[["Give an example of production feedback reaching developers.","An alert or dashboard owned by the team, or a trace linking an error to a recent deploy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-three-ways-value-streams-c3","t":"The Third Way - continual learning and experimentation","p":"Build a culture where people take calculated risks, practise failure and turn every incident into improvement. Repetition and practice build mastery.","k":[["What is a game day?","A planned exercise in which a team practises responding to a failure scenario."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-three-ways-value-streams-c4","t":"Value streams and value stream mapping","p":"A value stream is the sequence of steps from a request to the customer receiving value. A value stream map shows each step with its active time and waiting time.","k":[["What is flow efficiency?","The share of total lead time spent actively working rather than waiting."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-three-ways-value-streams-c5","t":"Theory of Constraints","p":"Every system has one bottleneck that limits its output. Improving anything else does not increase throughput; improving the bottleneck does.","k":[["Why not add developers when review is the bottleneck?","More code only lengthens the review queue; throughput stays at the review capacity."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-three-ways-value-streams-c6","t":"Unplanned work and the four types of work","p":"The Phoenix Project names four kinds of work: business projects, internal IT projects, changes, and unplanned work (firefighting). Unplanned work steals time from everything else.","k":[["Why track unplanned work?","It is invisible demand that crowds out improvement; knowing its size justifies investing in prevention."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s10-dora-metrics","title":"DORA metrics and delivery performance","c":[{"id":"s10-dora-metrics-c1","t":"The DORA metrics","p":"Five measures of software delivery. Throughput: lead time for changes, deployment frequency and failed deployment recovery time. Stability: change failure rate and (in recent reports) rework rate. Older material lists four keys with \"time to restore service\".","k":[["Which metrics measure stability?","Change failure rate and failed deployment recovery time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dora-metrics-c2","t":"Performance bands","p":"DORA groups teams into bands such as elite, high, medium and low depending on their numbers. The bands show what is possible, not a score to chase.","k":[["Should bands be used as team targets?","No. They are for orientation; setting them as targets invites gaming."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dora-metrics-c3","t":"Measuring in practice","p":"Collect the metrics automatically from the systems you already have: Git, CI/CD, deployment tooling and incident records.","k":[["What needs a clear definition before measuring change failure rate?","What counts as a failure, such as a rollback, a hotfix or a linked incident."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dora-metrics-c4","t":"Misuse and Goodhart's law","p":"When a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure. DORA metrics are diagnostic indicators for teams, not performance reviews.","k":[["Why show all four metrics together?","Optimising one alone, such as speed, can hide a drop in stability."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dora-metrics-c5","t":"Capabilities that drive performance","p":"DORA also identifies practices that cause better performance. They fall into technical, process and cultural capabilities.","k":[["Which is linked to better outcomes, an external change board or peer review inside the team?","Peer review with automated checks; external boards correlate with slower delivery and no stability benefit."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dora-metrics-c6","t":"Improving lead time step by step","p":"Break lead time into phases, find the largest waiting segment, remove it and measure again.","k":[["What is the usual first fix when most of lead time is waiting for release?","Automate the release and deploy continuously instead of batching."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s11-environments-config","title":"Environments and configuration","c":[{"id":"s11-environments-config-c1","t":"The environment ladder","p":"Changes move upward through environments: a developer machine, shared development, test or staging, and production. Each step is more realistic and more controlled.","k":[["Why use a preview environment per pull request?","Reviewers can test the change in isolation before it merges."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-environments-config-c2","t":"Environment parity","p":"Parity means non-production environments behave like production in the ways that matter: versions, topology, configuration shape and (safe) data.","k":[["Which should always be identical between staging and production?","The deployed artifact (image digest) and the way it is deployed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-environments-config-c3","t":"Configuration and the twelve-factor approach","p":"Configuration is everything that changes between environments: URLs, credentials, feature settings. It should live outside the build artifact and be injected at deploy or run time.","k":[["Why is baking config into images a problem?","It forces different builds per environment, so the tested artifact differs from the deployed one."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-environments-config-c4","t":"Secrets management","p":"Secrets are credentials: passwords, API keys, tokens and certificates. They need stricter handling than other config: no plain text in Git, tight access, rotation and audit.","k":[["Are Kubernetes Secrets encrypted by default?","No. They are base64 encoded in etcd unless encryption at rest or an external KMS provider is configured."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-environments-config-c5","t":"Test data and data protection","p":"Non-production environments need realistic data without exposing real customers. Use generated or masked data.","k":[["Why use synthetic data?","It carries no personal information, so there is no privacy risk and tests are repeatable."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-environments-config-c6","t":"Infrastructure as code for environments","p":"Define every environment in code (Terraform, Helm, Kustomize, GitOps), so creating or recreating one is a repeatable, reviewed operation.","k":[["What does GitOps add beyond running kubectl apply from a pipeline?","A controller continuously compares the cluster with Git and corrects drift."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags","title":"Release strategies, branching and feature flags","c":[{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags-c1","t":"Deploy versus release","p":"Deploying puts new code on servers. Releasing exposes the new behaviour to users. Separating the two removes most release-day risk.","k":[["Why is a flag rollback faster than a code rollback?","No build or deployment is needed; the new behaviour is simply turned off."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags-c2","t":"Rolling and recreate deployments","p":"A rolling update replaces old instances with new ones a few at a time. A recreate deployment stops all old instances and then starts the new ones.","k":[["What does maxUnavailable 0 guarantee?","Capacity never drops below the desired replica count during the update, because new pods must be ready first."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags-c3","t":"Blue-green deployments","p":"Run two complete environments: blue (current) and green (new). Test green, then switch traffic from blue to green in one step. Blue stays ready for instant rollback.","k":[["What is the main cost of blue-green?","Running two full environments at once, and keeping data schemas compatible."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags-c4","t":"Canary releases and progressive delivery","p":"Send a small percentage of real traffic to the new version, compare its health with the old one, and increase gradually or abort automatically.","k":[],"o":[["Rolling","most stateless services with backward compatible changes","two versions cannot run side by side"],["Blue-green","instant cutover and instant rollback matter","capacity is too costly to double"],["Canary","risky changes where real traffic is the best test","you lack metrics to judge health"],["Recreate","batch jobs or apps that need exclusive access","users cannot tolerate downtime"]]},{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags-c5","t":"Feature flags","p":"A feature flag is a runtime switch that turns behaviour on or off, for everyone or for chosen users, without deploying.","k":[["Why hash the user id rather than random per request?","The same user must always get the same experience."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags-c6","t":"Branching strategies","p":"A branching strategy defines how changes move from a developer to main to production. The main options are trunk-based development, GitHub Flow, GitFlow and release branches.","k":[["What does git cherry-pick -x add?","A line in the commit message naming the original commit, which keeps traceability."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-release-strategies-flags-c7","t":"Versioning and release notes","p":"A version number tells consumers what changed. Semantic versioning uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH: major for breaking changes, minor for compatible features, patch for compatible fixes.","k":[["What bump does a backwards-incompatible API change need?","A major version bump."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s13-test-pyramid","title":"Testing strategy and the test pyramid","c":[{"id":"s13-test-pyramid-c1","t":"The test pyramid","p":"Have many fast, small tests at the bottom, fewer integration tests in the middle and very few slow end-to-end tests at the top.","k":[["Why not test everything end to end?","It is slow, flaky and gives vague failures, so it is used only for critical journeys."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-test-pyramid-c2","t":"Unit tests that last","p":"A good unit test checks behaviour through the public interface, runs fast, is independent of other tests and fails for one clear reason.","k":[["What makes a unit test brittle?","Coupling to implementation details such as private methods or call order instead of observable behaviour."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-test-pyramid-c3","t":"Integration tests and test containers","p":"Integration tests check that your code works with real neighbours: a database, message queue or another service, not just mocks of them.","k":[["Why use a real database in integration tests?","Dialects, constraints and migrations behave differently from mocks and fail only against the real engine."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-test-pyramid-c4","t":"Contract testing for services","p":"A contract test checks that a consumer and a provider agree on the shape of an API without running both together in a full environment.","k":[["Who verifies a contract, the consumer or the provider?","The provider verifies in its own pipeline that it still meets what consumers published."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-test-pyramid-c5","t":"Flaky tests and test speed","p":"A flaky test sometimes passes and sometimes fails with no code change. Slow suites cause people to skip tests. Both erode trust.","k":[["Why should a flaky test be quarantined quickly?","Otherwise the team learns to ignore red builds, and real failures slip through."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-test-pyramid-c6","t":"Load, performance and resilience testing","p":"These tests check how the system behaves under stress: many users, large data, and failures of dependencies.","k":[["Where do pass criteria for a load test come from?","From your SLOs, for example latency percentiles and error rate."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-test-pyramid-c7","t":"Exploratory testing and quality ownership","p":"Automated checks confirm known expectations. People exploring the product find the surprises scripts do not look for.","k":[["What happens to a bug found by exploration?","It is fixed, and a regression test is added so it cannot return unnoticed."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s14-devsecops","title":"DevSecOps in the pipeline","c":[{"id":"s14-devsecops-c1","t":"Security checks along the pipeline","p":"Each stage of the pipeline can run a different kind of check. Together they catch problems from the editor to production.","k":[["What does SAST analyse?","Source code, without running it, to find insecure patterns."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-devsecops-c2","t":"SAST, SCA and DAST","p":"Three families of tests: SAST reads your code, SCA checks the third-party libraries you use, and DAST attacks the running application.","k":[["Why is SCA often the highest value scan?","Most of the code in an application is third-party, and known CVEs in it are the most commonly exploited weaknesses."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-devsecops-c3","t":"Triage: severity, exploitability and thresholds","p":"Scanners report thousands of findings. Triage decides which ones matter now, which can wait and which are not real.","k":[["Why combine severity with exploitability?","A critical CVE in unreachable code is less urgent than a medium one that is actively exploited and exposed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-devsecops-c4","t":"Threat modelling","p":"Threat modelling is a structured conversation: what are we building, what can go wrong, what will we do about it, and did we do a good job.","k":[["Where do you draw trust boundaries?","Wherever data crosses between zones of different trust, such as internet to cluster or service to database."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-devsecops-c5","t":"Policy as code","p":"Security and compliance rules are written as code and enforced automatically at pull request time and at deploy time.","k":[["Why start policies in audit mode?","To find existing violations without breaking running workloads, then enforce once they are fixed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-devsecops-c6","t":"Secrets and identity in the pipeline","p":"The CI system holds the keys to production, so it is a prime target. Give each job short-lived, narrowly scoped credentials.","k":[["What is the benefit of OIDC federation over stored access keys?","Credentials are short-lived and tied to a specific repository and branch, so there is nothing long-lived to steal."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-devsecops-c7","t":"Security culture and champions","p":"Tools find issues; people prevent them. A security champion in each team connects developers with the security group and spreads practice.","k":[["What is a security champion?","A developer in a product team who acts as the local link to security practice."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s15-supply-chain-sbom","title":"Software supply chain, SBOM and quality gates","c":[{"id":"s15-supply-chain-sbom-c1","t":"The supply chain and its attack points","p":"Your product depends on source code, libraries, build tools, base images, the CI system and the registry. An attacker can compromise any link.","k":[["Why use npm ci instead of npm install in CI?","It installs exactly what the lock file says and fails if it is out of sync."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-supply-chain-sbom-c2","t":"SBOM - software bill of materials","p":"An SBOM lists every component in a piece of software, with versions and licences, like an ingredients label.","k":[["Why scan an SBOM again after release?","New vulnerabilities are published daily, so a component that was clean at build time may be vulnerable now."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-supply-chain-sbom-c3","t":"Signing and verification","p":"A digital signature proves who built an artifact and that it was not changed after signing. Verification checks that proof before use.","k":[["Why sign a digest and not a tag?","Tags are mutable, so a signature on a tag could cover different content later."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-supply-chain-sbom-c4","t":"Provenance and SLSA","p":"Provenance is a signed record of how an artifact was built: source repository, commit, builder and build steps. SLSA (\"salsa\") is a framework that grades how trustworthy a build process is.","k":[["What does provenance prove that a signature alone does not?","How and where the artifact was built, from which source."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-supply-chain-sbom-c5","t":"Quality gates","p":"A quality gate is an automated rule in the pipeline that must pass before a change moves on: tests pass, no critical vulnerabilities, coverage not reduced, policy satisfied.","k":[["Why gate on new findings rather than all findings?","Old debt would block every change; gating on new problems stops things getting worse while allowing gradual cleanup."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-supply-chain-sbom-c6","t":"Code quality and technical standards","p":"Static analysis and review keep code understandable: consistent style, low complexity, and no obvious defects.","k":[["Why automate formatting?","It removes style debates from review and keeps diffs focused on logic."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget","title":"SRE principles, SLIs and SLOs","c":[{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget-c1","t":"SRE and DevOps","p":"DevOps is a set of cultural ideas; SRE is a concrete way to apply them to reliability, with specific practices such as SLOs, error budgets and toil limits. Google describes it as \"class SRE implements interface DevOps\".","k":[["Why not aim for 100 percent availability?","It is unreachable (users' own networks fail first), and each extra nine costs far more while slowing feature delivery."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget-c2","t":"SLIs - what to measure","p":"A service level indicator (SLI) is a measurement of one aspect of service quality, expressed as the proportion of good events out of valid events.","k":[["Why is a ratio of good to valid events preferred?","It is easy to read, ranges from 0 to 100 percent and works for any kind of event."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget-c3","t":"SLOs and SLAs","p":"A service level objective (SLO) is the target for an SLI over a window, for example 99.9 percent of requests succeed over 30 days. An SLA is a contract with penalties, usually looser than the internal SLO.","k":[["Why should the SLO be stricter than the SLA?","It triggers action before the contractual penalty is reached."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget-c4","t":"Error budgets","p":"The error budget is the allowed amount of unreliability: 100 percent minus the SLO. A 99.9 percent SLO gives a 0.1 percent budget, about 43 minutes in 30 days.","k":[["How many minutes of full outage does 99.9 percent allow in 30 days?","About 43.2 minutes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget-c5","t":"Burn rate alerting","p":"Burn rate says how fast you are consuming the error budget. A burn rate of 1 uses the whole budget exactly at the end of the window; 10 uses it ten times faster.","k":[["What does burn rate 1 mean?","Errors are using the budget at exactly the pace that exhausts it at the end of the window."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget-c6","t":"Choosing good SLOs","p":"SLOs should reflect what users care about, be achievable and be few. Starting rough and improving beats waiting for the perfect SLO.","k":[["Why can a service not exceed the availability of its hard dependencies?","If any hard dependency fails the service fails too, so availabilities multiply."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-sre-slo-error-budget-c7","t":"Observability that supports SLOs","p":"Metrics, logs and traces let you measure SLIs and diagnose why they drop. Observability is the ability to ask new questions of a running system without deploying new code.","k":[["Which signal shows the path of one request through many services?","Traces."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents","title":"Toil, on-call and incident response","c":[{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents-c1","t":"Toil","p":"Toil is operational work that is manual, repetitive, automatable, tactical, without lasting value and grows as the service grows. Restarting a stuck job by hand every week is toil. Designing a fix is engineering.","k":[["Is a weekly planning meeting toil?","No, toil is repetitive operational work that could be automated; meetings are overhead."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents-c2","t":"Automating toil away","p":"Turn recurring manual steps into code: scripts first, then self-healing systems, then removing the cause.","k":[["What is the last rung of the automation ladder?","Eliminating the cause through design, so no fix or automation is needed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents-c3","t":"On-call that people can sustain","p":"On-call means being available to respond to alerts outside normal hours. Done well it is shared, paid or compensated, and rare enough to avoid burnout.","k":[["Which alerts should page a human?","Those showing user-visible symptoms that need immediate action; the rest become tickets."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents-c4","t":"Alert quality","p":"A good alert is urgent, actionable, and tied to user impact. A bad alert is noise that teaches people to ignore the pager.","k":[["What should every alert include?","A runbook link, an owner and a severity."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents-c5","t":"Incident response roles","p":"A major incident is handled by a small team with defined roles, so decisions are clear and nobody works in parallel on contradictory fixes.","k":[["Why should the incident commander not debug?","They must keep an overview and coordinate; deep debugging loses that view."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents-c6","t":"Severity levels and the incident lifecycle","p":"Severity levels classify the impact, so the response is proportionate. The lifecycle is detect, triage, mitigate, resolve, learn.","k":[["Why mitigate before finding root cause?","Users are affected now; restoring service reduces harm, and diagnosis can continue calmly afterwards."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-toil-oncall-incidents-c7","t":"Communication during incidents","p":"Good communication keeps stakeholders informed and engineers free to work. Use one channel, regular updates and plain language.","k":[["What goes in a status update?","Impact, current knowledge, current action and the time of the next update."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s18-postmortems-capacity","title":"Postmortems, capacity and reliability practice","c":[{"id":"s18-postmortems-capacity-c1","t":"Blameless postmortems","p":"A postmortem is a written review after an incident: what happened, why, how we responded and what we will change. It is blameless, so people can describe facts honestly.","k":[["Why record where we got lucky?","Luck hides risks that could have made the incident worse next time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-postmortems-capacity-c2","t":"Root cause and contributing factors","p":"Incidents rarely have a single cause. Several small weaknesses line up. Analysis looks for all of them rather than stopping at the first \"human error\".","k":[["Why is \"be more careful\" a poor action item?","It relies on human memory and attention; stronger fixes remove the chance of the error."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-postmortems-capacity-c3","t":"Tracking actions and learning across incidents","p":"A postmortem only helps if its action items are done. Track them like any other work and look for patterns across incidents.","k":[["Where should postmortem actions be tracked?","In the same backlog and process as other engineering work, with owners and dates."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-postmortems-capacity-c4","t":"Capacity planning","p":"Capacity planning makes sure there are enough resources for expected load plus headroom, without paying for large idle surplus.","k":[["What does N+1 capacity protect against?","The loss of one unit (a node or zone) while still carrying the peak load."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-postmortems-capacity-c5","t":"Graceful degradation and resilience patterns","p":"When part of the system fails or load is too high, a resilient service keeps its core function working and sheds or simplifies the rest.","k":[["Why add jitter to retries?","It spreads retries over time, so many clients do not hit the recovering service at the same instant."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-postmortems-capacity-c6","t":"Game days, chaos engineering and disaster recovery","p":"Practise failure on purpose, in a controlled way, so recovery steps work when you need them. Disaster recovery (DR) covers losing a whole region or data store.","k":[["What belongs in a chaos experiment before it starts?","A hypothesis, a limited blast radius and an abort condition."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs","title":"Requirements, user stories and architecture documentation","c":[{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c1","t":"Functional and non functional requirements","p":"A functional requirement says what the system does (\"a customer can reset a password\"). A non functional requirement says how well it does it: speed, availability, security, cost.","k":[["Which kind of requirement drives the choice of multi zone deployment?","Non functional, specifically the availability target."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c2","t":"User stories and the INVEST checklist","p":"A user story states a need from the user's point of view: \"As a role, I want a goal, so that a benefit\". It starts a conversation instead of replacing it.","k":[["Which INVEST letter does \"split it until it fits in a few days\" serve?","Small."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c3","t":"Acceptance criteria and Given When Then","p":"Acceptance criteria are concrete examples that must pass for the story to be done. Given When Then (Gherkin) is a readable format: the starting state, the action, the expected result.","k":[["Why include unhappy paths in acceptance criteria?","Because most defects and production incidents arise from errors and edge cases."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c4","t":"Turning quality into numbers","p":"Words like \"fast\", \"reliable\" and \"secure\" cannot be tested. A measurable target names the metric, the level, the load and the time window.","k":[["Why use p95 instead of the average?","Averages hide the slow tail that real users experience."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c5","t":"The C4 model: context and containers","p":"C4 describes software at four zoom levels: Context, Containers, Components, Code. Each diagram answers one question for one audience.","k":[["At which C4 level do you show the database?","Level 2, containers."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c6","t":"The C4 model: components and code","p":"Level 3 zooms into one container and shows its components (controllers, services, repositories). Level 4 is the code itself, usually generated from source, and rarely drawn by hand.","k":[["Who is the audience of level 1?","Everyone, including non technical stakeholders."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c7","t":"Architecture Decision Records","p":"An ADR is a short document recording one significant decision: the context, the options considered, the choice and its consequences.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-requirements-architecture-docs-c8","t":"Documentation as code","p":"Keep documentation in the same repository and review process as the code, in plain text formats, built and published by the pipeline.","k":[["Why keep docs in the code repository?","They are reviewed and versioned with the change, so they stay accurate."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt","title":"Code review, trunk based development and technical debt","c":[{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c1","t":"Why and how to review code","p":"A code review is a colleague reading a change before it merges. The goal is shared understanding and catching problems early, not gatekeeping.","k":[["Why automate formatting and linting in review?","So reviewers spend attention on design and risk rather than style."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c2","t":"Giving and receiving review feedback","p":"Review comments are about the code, not the person. The tone decides whether a team learns from review or avoids it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c3","t":"Pair and mob programming","p":"In pair programming two people work on one task at one screen: a driver types, a navigator thinks ahead. In mob programming the whole team does this together.","k":[["When is pairing most valuable?","Onboarding, complex design and unfamiliar code where shared knowledge pays back."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c4","t":"Trunk based development","p":"Everyone integrates into one main branch (the trunk) at least daily. Branches live for hours or a day or two. Unfinished features hide behind feature flags.","k":[["How do you merge half-finished work to trunk safely?","Behind a feature flag that is off until the feature is ready."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c5","t":"Branch protection and merge strategies","p":"Rules on the main branch keep it releasable: required reviews, required passing checks, no force pushes. Merge strategy controls the history shape.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c6","t":"Monorepo versus many repositories","p":"A monorepo holds many projects in one repository. A polyrepo setup gives each project its own. Both work; each has costs.","k":[["What lets a monorepo stay fast?","Selective builds and caching, so only affected projects are built and tested."]],"o":[["Monorepo","tightly related services, shared libraries, one team or strong platform tooling","very large unrelated codebases with no build tooling"],["Polyrepo","independent teams, separate release cadence, strong API contracts","frequent cross-service changes"]]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c7","t":"What technical debt is and how to measure it","p":"Technical debt is the future cost of shortcuts and outdated design. Like financial debt it has principal (work to fix) and interest (extra effort every time you touch the area).","k":[["What is the \"interest\" of technical debt?","The extra time and risk paid every time the area is changed or operated."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-code-review-trunk-tech-debt-c8","t":"Paying down debt deliberately","p":"Debt is managed, not eliminated. Reserve capacity for it, tie it to goals, and make the improvement boy-scout small where possible.","k":[["Why use a strangler pattern instead of a rewrite?","It delivers value incrementally, keeps the old system working and limits risk."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident","title":"Platform engineering, change management and incident command","c":[{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident-c1","t":"Platform engineering and the internal developer platform","p":"An internal developer platform (IDP) is a set of self-service tools and defaults, built by a platform team, that lets developers create, deploy and run services without being infrastructure experts.","k":[["What distinguishes platform engineering from a central ops ticket queue?","Self-service, treated as a product with customers and metrics."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident-c2","t":"Golden paths","p":"A golden path is the supported, well-lit route for a common task, such as building a web service: the template, pipeline, deployment settings and monitoring already wired together.","k":[["Is a golden path mandatory?","No; it is the easiest supported path, with an escape hatch for special needs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident-c3","t":"Backstage and the software catalog","p":"Backstage is an open source developer portal. Its core is a software catalog: a searchable inventory of services, owners, APIs, docs and dependencies, described by YAML files in each repository.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident-c4","t":"Change management: ITIL versus DevOps","p":"Change management controls how changes reach production to limit risk. Traditional ITIL processes use a Change Advisory Board (CAB) that reviews requests. DevOps replaces most manual approval with automated evidence and small, frequent changes.","k":[["How does DevOps keep an audit trail without a CAB?","Git history, pull request approvals and pipeline logs record who changed what and what checks passed."]],"o":[["Pre-approved standard change","routine, well-tested, reversible, automated deploys","schema changes without rollback"],["Peer review plus automated gates","most application changes","regulated change requiring segregation of duties"],["Human CAB or risk review","high blast radius, novel, irreversible changes","frequent routine releases"]]},{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident-c5","t":"Incident command: roles and structure","p":"For a serious incident, a small set of defined roles replaces chaos: one person leads, one communicates, others investigate. The model comes from emergency services (Incident Command System).","k":[["Should the incident commander debug?","No; the IC coordinates so someone keeps the whole picture."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident-c6","t":"Incident communication","p":"Good communication is part of the fix. Stakeholders, customers and teammates need regular, honest, plain-language updates, even when the update is \"no change\".","k":[["What should every update contain, even with no news?","The next update time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-platform-change-incident-c7","t":"On call health and learning from incidents","p":"Reliable systems depend on rested people. A sustainable on-call rotation, strong handoffs and a blameless review process turn incidents into improvement.","k":[["Why is a blameless postmortem more effective?","People share honest detail, which reveals system causes that can be fixed."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases","title":"FinOps, developer experience, careers and case studies","c":[{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c1","t":"The FinOps cycle","p":"FinOps is a culture and practice of making cloud spending a shared engineering concern. Engineers, finance and product work from the same data and make trade offs between speed, quality and cost.","k":[["Which phase includes buying commitments?","Optimise."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c2","t":"Cost allocation: tags, accounts and Kubernetes labels","p":"Allocation assigns each dollar to a team, product or environment. Without it, the bill is one big number nobody owns.","k":[["Why is Kubernetes allocation harder than per-VM allocation?","Pods from many teams share nodes, so cost must be split by requests or usage."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c3","t":"Rightsizing Kubernetes workloads","p":"Rightsizing adjusts CPU and memory requests to what workloads actually need. Over-requesting reserves capacity you pay for but do not use.","k":[["Why not rightsize to exactly the average usage?","Peaks, zone failures and growth need headroom; use a high percentile plus margin."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c4","t":"Commitments, spot and unit economics","p":"After removing waste, reduce the price per unit with commitments and cheaper capacity, and track unit economics: cost per order, per active user or per request.","k":[["Spend rose 20 percent and orders rose 50 percent. Is efficiency better or worse?","Better: cost per order fell 20 percent."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c5","t":"Measuring developer experience with SPACE","p":"Developer experience (DevEx) is how it feels to build and ship software here: fast feedback, low friction, clear goals. The SPACE framework (Forsgren and colleagues, 2021) says productivity has five dimensions and no single metric captures it.","k":[["Why combine survey data with system data?","Systems show what happens, surveys show how it feels and why; both are needed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c6","t":"DevEx drivers and relation to DORA","p":"Research on developer experience (Noda, Storey, Forsgren, Greiler) groups the main drivers into three: feedback loops, cognitive load and flow state. DORA's four key metrics measure delivery outcomes; SPACE and DevEx explain what helps or hinders the people producing them.","k":[["Which driver does a golden path mainly reduce?","Cognitive load."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c7","t":"Career ladders for DevOps and platform engineers","p":"A career ladder describes what is expected at each level: scope, autonomy and impact. Titles differ by company, but the pattern is similar.","k":[["What changes most between engineer and senior?","Scope: from a component to a system and other people's growth."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-finops-devex-careers-cases-c8","t":"Case studies and lessons","p":"Real incidents and migrations teach more than theory. These three composite scenarios are modelled on common public postmortems and are not about any specific company.","k":[["What was common to all three cases?","The failure was predictable and preventable with automation, staged change and observability."]],"o":[]}]}]};